  {
    "id": "10.1016/j.matt.2026.102676",
    "venue": "j-0a94e1dd0b",
    "year": 2026,
    "track": null,
    "title": "Absorption dissymmetry factor enhancement: A data-driven approach to unravel the synthesis knobs of chiral 2D perovskites",
    "authors": [
      "Raphael F. Moral",
      "Maher B. Alghalayini",
      "Raushan N. Nurdillayeva",
      "Do-Kyoung Lee",
      "Tim Kodalle",
      "Paulo E. Marchezi",
      "David P. Fenning",
      "Marcus M. Noack",
      "Craig P. Schwartz",
      "Carolin M. Sutter-Fella"
    ],
    "author_ids": [
      "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1844-4035"
    ],
    "abstract": "Chiral 2D metal halide perovskites (MHPs) hold promise for next-generation spin-optoelectronic applications. While still in early-stage development, significant efforts have focused on optimizing the dissymmetry factor ( g ), particularly in terms of their circular dichroism ( g abs ), which quantifies the chiroptical response related to the differential absorption of left and right circularly-polarized light. These materials typically exhibit intrinsically low g abs values (10 -3 ), with variations spanning two orders of magnitude due to co-dependent structural, morphological, and experimental factors. Here, Pearson correlation analysis identified solvent choice as the primary factor governing g abs variability in chiral 2D MHP thin films based on R -/ S -𝛼-methylbenzylammonium. Subsequently, a combination of Pearson correlation, ANOVA, and Gaussian process regression revealed that, for acetonitrile-based samples, film thickness and annealing temperature are the key parameters for optimizing g abs . In contrast, films prepared with higher boiling point solvents exhibit a more complex interdependence of g abs with annealing temperature, excitonic integral intensity, and film texture. The statistical analysis of the synthesis-chiroptical property relationship provides a foundation for the rational design of chiral 2D MHPs, while also guiding the exploration of alternative chiral cations, compositions, and deposition strategies.",
